
export anchor CloseSprintAlwaysReachableOnceTheSprint2:
    userPrompt:
        \\\
        Once a sprint is done, it can always be closed. Read [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint), [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start) and [States](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#states) first.

        Today "Close sprint" only appears on Current sprint's heading once every story there has ended. A story whose task can't be found reads as Queued forever (`status` in src/planning.rs). A story that was never sent, because `send_named` skipped its empty prompt, has no task at all. Either one keeps `sprint_ended` false, so the sprint can never be closed. The spec has nothing on either case yet, so this part is new. Specify it in [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint) and then make the code match:

        - A story in Current sprint whose task no longer exists counts as ended and not Done. Its row shows that its task is gone (not "Queued"), and Close sprint sends it back to the start of Next sprint, as it does with failed and cancelled stories.
        - Send sprint never moves a story it didn't send into Current sprint. A story with an empty title and prompt stays in Next sprint.
        - [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start) treats a story with a missing task as it treats a failed one: the next sprint doesn't start by itself, and the switch's row says why.
        - Closing still saves the sections to `order.json`, as [Project Data](.claude/reference/scope/project-data/index.md#contents) says. After a reload or restart, a finished sprint can still be closed.
        \\\

    mode: code

    newConversation: false

    systemPrompt:
        \\\
        We're working on the code, located in ./src. Change the code so it does what the prompt asks, as the spec describes it.

        When the work is about Suspense, its modes, its prompts, or its questions, or you are to write a prompt or a question for the user, first read .suspense/suspense-fluency.md once in this conversation, unless you already have: it is how Suspense works.

        The spec's Piton source is in ./spec. Don't change it, and don't read it to learn what the spec says: read the compiled reference instead, as above. If the prompt asks for something the spec doesn't describe, or describes differently, say so in your reply rather than working around it.

        Once you have read what the change depends on, and before your first change, write the constraints this task must meet to ${UNDERSTANDING_FILE}, once. Rewrite it only when a constraint in it turns out to be wrong, never after each page you read. It is a Markdown list and nothing else: one line per constraint, at most twelve, each a single short, specific statement of what must be true, written as a link to the compiled reference file it comes from, such as `- [The chain tab has no bottom border in combined mode](.claude/reference/scope/prompt-mode/chat-input/index.md#chain)`. Only list what the spec says; leave out anything you are deciding yourself. Replace a constraint when you learn it was wrong rather than adding another.

        End with a reply of a few sentences saying what changed, or for a question, the answer, not a recap of the work or of what you read.

        This prompt is the second step of a chain. Its first step changed the spec to meet it, and the spec has been built since. Now change the code so it does what the spec now says, without changing the spec. Follow the spec the prompt references, and the anchors the spec step names below. Where the spec and the prompt disagree, follow the spec, and say so in your reply.

        The code is located in ./src.

        The spec's source, which this step doesn't change, is in ./spec.

        The prompt the chain was sent:

        ${SPEC_PROMPT}

        What the spec step said it changed, its final output:

        ${SPEC_RESULT}
        \\\

    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            Once a sprint is done, it can always be closed. Read [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint), [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start) and [States](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#states) first.

            Today "Close sprint" only appears on Current sprint's heading once every story there has ended. A story whose task can't be found reads as Queued forever (`status` in src/planning.rs). A story that was never sent, because `send_named` skipped its empty prompt, has no task at all. Either one keeps `sprint_ended` false, so the sprint can never be closed. The spec has nothing on either case yet, so this part is new. Specify it in [Sprint](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint) and then make the code match:

            - A story in Current sprint whose task no longer exists counts as ended and not Done. Its row shows that its task is gone (not "Queued"), and Close sprint sends it back to the start of Next sprint, as it does with failed and cancelled stories.
            - Send sprint never moves a story it didn't send into Current sprint. A story with an empty title and prompt stays in Next sprint.
            - [Auto Start](.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#auto-start) treats a story with a missing task as it treats a failed one: the next sprint doesn't start by itself, and the switch's row says why.
            - Closing still saves the sections to `order.json`, as [Project Data](.claude/reference/scope/project-data/index.md#contents) says. After a reload or restart, a finished sprint can still be closed.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            A finished sprint can now always be closed, even when a story's task has gone missing or was never sent. `piton check` reports no problems and `piton build` wrote 86 files.

            **Sprint** (`.claude/reference/scope/planning/index.md#sprint`):
            - **Empty stories stay put:** Send sprint only moves the stories it actually sent into Current sprint. A story with an empty title and prompt stays in Next sprint, and a muted note such as "1 empty story left in Next sprint" says why. If every story is empty, nothing is sent and no Current sprint appears.
            - **Missing tasks count as ended:** a story whose task can't be found is treated as ended but not Done. That covers a deleted record, a task never recorded, or one lost when the app quit. Its row reads "Task missing" in the warning colour, never "Queued".
            - **When Close sprint shows:** as soon as no story is building, compiling, running, paused, queued or held. It never waits on a task that can't arrive.
            - **Where missing ones go:** Close sprint sends them back to the start of Next sprint, like failed and cancelled stories, never into Done.
            - **After a reload or restart:** closing saves every section to `order.json` straight away. Current sprint's state is worked out again from its tasks whenever the plan is read, so a sprint that finished before you reloaded can still be closed.

            **Auto Start** (`#auto-start`): a missing task blocks the next sprint from starting by itself, the same as a failure, and the switch's row says why.

            **States** (`#states`): "Task missing" is added to the list of story statuses.

            **Anchors the code should follow:** `Planning` `#sprint`, `#auto-start` and `#states`, and `ProjectData` `#contents`. In `src/planning.rs`:
            - `status` must return a missing-task state instead of Queued.
            - `sprint_ended` must count missing tasks as ended.
            - `send_named` must not move skipped empty stories into Current sprint.
            \\\

    sentFrom: CloseSprintAlwaysReachableOnceTheSprint

    model: opus
